---
q: what time is it
a: date
out: [0-9]
ref: date
teach: date
---
q: the time in utc rather than local time
a: date -u
out: UTC|[0-9]
ref: date
teach: date -u
---
q: today's date as YYYY-MM-DD and nothing else
a: date +%Y-%m-%d
out: ^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}$
ref: date
teach: date
---
q: the current time as seconds since 1970
a: date +%s
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: date
teach: date
---
q: the date in iso 8601 form using the flag rather than a format string
a: date -I
out: ^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}$
ref: date
teach: date -I[SPEC]
---
q: the date in rfc 2822 form, the one email headers use
a: date -R
out: [0-9]
ref: date
teach: date -R
---
q: the modification time of @txt@ rather than the time now
a: date -r @txt@
out: [0-9]
ref: date
teach: date -r
---
q: interpret a date string rather than reading the clock
a: date -d 2024-03-01
out: 2024|Mar
ref: date
teach: date -d
---
q: parse a date given in an explicit input format
a: date -D "%Y-%m-%d" -d 2024-03-01 +%s
ref: date
teach: date -D
---
q: how long has this machine been up, straight from the kernel
a: cat /proc/uptime
out: ^[0-9]
ref: cat
teach: cat
hint: /proc holds files that are not on any disk
---
q: what processor does this machine have, from /proc
a: cat /proc/cpuinfo
out: .
ref: cat
teach: cat
---
q: how much memory, from /proc
a: cat /proc/meminfo
out: Mem
ref: cat
teach: cat
---
q: what command line was this kernel booted with
a: cat /proc/cmdline
out: .
ref: cat
teach: cat
---
q: what filesystems are mounted, read from /proc rather than asking mount
a: cat /proc/mounts
out: /
ref: cat
teach: cat
---
